Revision history for Linux-Event-Async

0.002  2026-09-05
    - First stable release following the 0.001_001 developer version.
    - Require Linux::Event 0.110 and use its public resource hierarchy and
      Linux::Event::Framer native-consumer extension boundary rather than
      historical implementation package names.
    - Add Stream ready(), returning a Linux::Event::Async::Future that resolves
      with the same Stream at application readiness. Outbound readiness covers
      resolution, connect, and TLS handshake/verification where applicable.
    - Preserve normal core on_ready behavior alongside ready(): on_ready runs
      first, multiple Future waiters may observe the same one-shot readiness
      transition, and cancelling one readiness wait does not close the Stream.
    - Propagate connection, resolver, transport, TLS, timeout, and setup failures
      through pending readiness Futures using the Stream's Linux::Event::Error;
      explicitly closing before readiness produces an event/ready error.
    - Add Stream drain(), returning a Future that waits for an active
      high-water backpressure period to clear through the configured low
      watermark. It resolves at the normal Linux::Event drain transition rather
      than requiring pending_bytes to reach zero.
    - Reserve the Async Stream on_drain bridge so core callback dispatch and
      Future completion share one supported boundary. Drain Future cancellation
      is waiter-local and close/error fails pending drain waiters.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Listener with persistent awaitable accept().
      Acceptance is pull-based and paused while no wait is armed; cancelling an
      accept wait does not close the Listener.
    - Use one level-triggered kernel accept per armed Async Listener wait in
      0.002 so additional connections remain in the kernel backlog instead of
      being accepted behind an already completed Awaitable.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Timer with a persistent wait() Awaitable over the
      public Linux::Event::Kernel::Timer scheduler. Repeated recurring waits do
      not allocate one Future or Awaitable per tick.
    - Preserve Timer fixed-rate/coalesced semantics by returning the represented
      expiration count. Expirations occurring while no wait is armed accumulate
      in one scalar rather than an unbounded tick queue.
    - Make Timer wait cancellation wait-local: cancelling the Awaitable or an
      async sub currently awaiting it leaves the underlying recurring Timer
      active. Core Timer cancel remains terminal and fails a pending wait.
    - Synchronize the vendored ordered-byte consumer ABI v1 header with the
      released Linux::Event contract, including read-side terminal events and
      host retain/release lifetime operations.
    - Guard callback-capable consumer host resume and pause calls with the ABI
      retain/release contract so reentrant Stream close and destruction are
      safe.
    - Add deterministic regression coverage for reentrant Stream close from a
      receive cancellation callback.
    - Return one persistent native Awaitable view from Stream recv, avoiding one
      Future or Awaitable allocation per received message.
    - Keep receive arming in XS and use a bounded 64-message, approximately
      256 KiB prefetch ring. Consecutive receives can consume retained messages
      immediately while bounded pause and backpressure remain intact.
    - Add regression coverage for Stream readiness and drain, Listener accept,
      Timer waits and cancellation, multiple async subs sharing one Loop,
      out-of-order completion, isolated failure/cancellation, global
      destruction, and stress lifecycles.
    - Follow the currently awaited Linux::Event Loop when an async sub composes
      sequential operations across different Loops, including through nested
      child async subs, so AWAIT_WAIT does not remain blocked on an earlier
      Loop.
    - Make native consumer teardown safe during Perl global destruction.
    - Run release CI against the minimum supported Linux::Event 0.110 on Perl
      5.36 and 5.44 with make test and make disttest, plus a separate integration
      lane against current Linux::Event main.
    - Declare the Test2::V0 dependency required by the Future::AsyncAwait
      alternative-Awaitable conformance suite.
    - Add explicit CPAN provides/no_index metadata, repository resources, a
      Linux-only configure guard, and the Perl 5 license.
    - Rewrite public POD, README, and roadmap for the released architecture,
      including subclass framing/TLS policy, all Async-applicable Stream tuning
      options, readiness, drain, Listener accept, Timer waits, receive
      cancellation, bounded prefetch, and first-release scope.

0.001_001  2026-08-30
    - Initial development skeleton for the separate Linux::Event::Async
      distribution.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async import wrapper for Future::AsyncAwait.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Stream reusable receive Awaitable using the
      Linux::Event native Stream consumer ABI.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Future for async-sub result Futures.
    - Vendor the Linux::Event consumer ABI v1 header.
    - Return one persistent native Awaitable view from Stream recv, removing
      Stream hash lookup from the hot AWAIT_* methods.
    - Move Stream recv into XS so receive arming and Awaitable return no longer
      cross a Perl method trampoline.
    - Deduplicate repeated cancellation chaining to the persistent Stream
      Awaitable, keeping cancellation retention bounded across receive loops.
    - Batch native delivery behind the consumer ABI end-of-drain hook and a
      bounded 64-message, 256 KiB prefetch ring. Consecutive receives consume
      retained messages immediately instead of suspending through FAA once per
      frame, while consumer pause continues to provide bounded backpressure.
    - Add opt-in receive-path counters and separate receiver/producer CPU
      affinity controls to the matched native Stream benchmark.
